#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055660} :activity/id #uuid "2d0c79d2-d7c8-5c21-8622-ee767a7a7c30" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050843 :topic/id #uuid "6ae775b6-8adc-58c2-abd8-8374a1314249" :topic/math-academy-id 68} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055937 :tutorial/content "If a rational expression is expressed in factored form, we can sometimes make the expression even simpler by pulling out factors that are common in both the numerator and denominator.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to simplify the rational expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4(2x-3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we decompose $4$ into $2\\cdot 2,$ we can pull out a factor of $2$ from both the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fraction $\\dfrac{2}{2}$ is the same as $1,$ and anything times $1$ is just itself, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} \\\\ 2(2x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** There is a shortcut! We can just \"cancel\" a $2$ from the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{\\cancel{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ 2(2x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a1da69d-c5e3-5832-9f42-e75315b6c1a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1369 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f4f7f7d8-d9a6-5429-90e3-be9b91605346" :step/math-academy-id 2840 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055779}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054690 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054590 :question/id #uuid "3b2b92fa-7945-5609-8cc4-7ad230d396df" :ques